[Music]  hi my name is Richard I'm the owner of  Whole Foods here in East Village so we  make Taiwanese traditional beef noodle  soup in Taiwan there's many different  kinds you know a beef soup shop are like  pizza parlors in New York City every  neighborhood has one we make ours very  homestyle that's red cooked or fermented  broad bean paste raw sugar our own blend  of spices that dissin Oland warming you  know this dish was originally from the  soldiers who came from parts of China  and Szechuan you know they wanted a  taste of home and originally there was a  noodles in there was a beef dish but  there was so poor that they had to add  some doodles to make a dish that was of  sustenance and after the first phone was  made became so popular now it's the  national dish of Taiwan the most  important part about making beef noodle  soup it's just a lot of patience you  know soup is a slow process we start off  we use mostly shin bones you know we let  that go for an entire day and then  afterwards we add oro blend of spices a  star anis cloves such on better corns  cinnamon goji berries white peppercorns  black peppercorns and a few other things  in there too we actually cook the beef  separately with aromatics the Asian  Trinity ginger garlic onions fermented  broad bean paste rock sugar gives it a  nice glistening texture and look we put  a couple different types of soy sauce  aged soy sauce and dark soy sauce and  then from there we add in the beef  shanks you stir-fry it you know it cooks  the outside of the meat a little bit you  know it helps Brown it just a little bit  and then we add a little bit of water we  braise it and one of the most important  parts about that process is that after  you cook it you let it chill you know  you really want to let it settle and  reabsorb all the liquid that's been  braised and to keep it juicy and  flavorful and then from there we cook  noodles you know Tommy salvete noodles  are are very different than Phan doodles  or ramen noodles you know Tommy noodles  are made with flour water salt that's it  and what you're really looking for  there's a silky texture and you want it  to be chewy  we add in  a beef tallow and bone marrow mixture we  pull the noodle out put it in the beef  mixture we toss them then we add our  scallions then we add our meat then we  add this soup on a garnish it and try to  do as quickly as possible to get in the  kitchen to your table that's it I grew  up in Los Angeles  more specifically San Gabriel Valley you  know it's a place where when Pizza Hut  goes out of business you know a Chinese  restaurant goes in and I'm not  exaggerating there's maybe a thousand  Chinese restaurants where I grew up with  and you know we had really authentic  Tony's restaurants you know different  types whether it was a quick stir-fry or  a noodle shop and not knowing much about  American food besides like going to edit  out or something like that so we really  just kind of embedded and soaked in the  Asian culture you know this is how we  grew up eating and we wanted to share  that with everyone in New York City as  best we can  you  [Music]

